From 78aaaceca6956b5fb56e35feae57a0df536b32ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 19:18:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] A search that remembers, closest homes when nothing matches,
 a circle you can resize, and quicker clicks

- The AI search keeps this visit's last four searches. A follow-up (cheaper, add a
  pool, what about Conway) changes the last search; a different request starts over.
  It can now also exclude features, name a neighborhood or ZIP, ask for just-listed
  homes, waterfront, or newest first. It never uses the land-lease or rent-to-own
  filters unless the buyer says lease, lot rent or rent to own (a land search had
  come back as land leases). 'Land to build a house on' is land, and a word like
  'cheaper' is never read as a neighborhood.
- Nothing matches: our drawing and 'I'm sorry, no homes match all of that.', then
  the closest homes, with what was left out (features first, then the numbers, then
  all but the kind of home and the place).
- The search circle: drag its edge or middle, or use - and + above the map; each
  change searches again. The x removes it.
- A brass bar shows the moment a link is clicked. Signed-in pages are private,
  no-cache instead of no-store, so the back button can show them at once. For a
  visitor not signed in, the browser loads a home's page while the pointer rests
  on its link.
